; Schema-compile family, 1 of 4 — these four are rejections of the *schema*,
; raised before any document is looked at. `(track :kind kick)` below is
; well-formed against what the author meant; it is there only so the case
; reads as a document rather than a bare manifest.
;
; `:kind` is typed as the builtin `symbol`, which admits any symbol at all.
; A discriminant has to enumerate its variants, so the manifest is rejected
; at load: with an open type there is no set of `(variant :when …)` values to
; check against, and every later discriminant diagnostic would be noise on
; top of this one. That is why the compile stops here — the emitter returns
; straight after this diagnostic rather than going on to check the variants.
(plugin :name probe :version "1.0.0"
  (form :name track
    :discriminant kind
    (key :name kind :type symbol :optional true)
    (variant :when kick
      (key :name step :type number :optional true))))

(track :kind kick)
